<article class="env-lemma" id="014F">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/014F">Lemma <span data-tag="014F">13.9.2</span>.</a> Suppose that </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ K_1^\bullet \ar[r]_{f_1} \ar[d]_ a &  L_1^\bullet \ar[d]^ b \\  K_2^\bullet \ar[r]^{f_2} &  L_2^\bullet }  \]
</div>
  <p> is a diagram of morphisms of complexes which is commutative up to homotopy. Then there exists a morphism $c : C(f_1)^\bullet \to C(f_2)^\bullet $ which gives rise to a morphism of triangles $(a, b, c) : (K_1^\bullet , L_1^\bullet , C(f_1)^\bullet , f_1, i_1, p_1) \to (K_2^\bullet , L_2^\bullet , C(f_2)^\bullet , f_2, i_2, p_2)$ of $K(\mathcal{A})$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $h^ n : K_1^ n \to L_2^{n - 1}$ be a family of morphisms such that $b \circ f_1 - f_2 \circ a= d \circ h + h \circ d$. Define $c^ n$ by the matrix 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  c^ n = \left( \begin{matrix}  b^ n 

&  h^{n + 1} 

\\  0 

&  a^{n + 1} 

\end{matrix} \right) : L_1^ n \oplus K_1^{n + 1} \to L_2^ n \oplus K_2^{n + 1}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       A matrix computation show that $c$ is a morphism of complexes. It is trivial that $c \circ i_1 = i_2 \circ b$, and it is trivial also to check that $p_2 \circ c = a \circ p_1$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>